{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 21, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e4b-it", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli ruled the County of Tripoli from 1154 until his death in 1199. He was a significant figure in the Crusades and a powerful ruler in the Levant during a turbulent period. His reign was marked by military campaigns diplomatic maneuvering and efforts to consolidate his control over the region.\n\n*Early Life and Ascension* Raymond was born around 1110 the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Alice of Weissenburg. He inherited the County of Tripoli upon his father's death in 1154. This inheritance placed him in a strategically important location between the Byzantine Empire and the Islamic world. His early years were spent dealing with internal challenges and external threats including attacks from Muslim forces and rival Crusader states. He faced opposition from various factions within his own county and had to assert his authority to maintain stability.\n\nRaymond III engaged in numerous military campaigns throughout his reign. He fought against the Zengids a powerful Muslim dynasty that controlled much of Syria. He also clashed with other Crusader rulers such as Raymond of Antioch and Baldwin III of Jerusalem. His military successes helped to secure the borders of the County of Tripoli and maintain its independence. He participated in the Third Crusade though his involvement was relatively limited compared to other Crusader leaders. His focus remained on defending his own territory and maintaining his position as a regional power.\n\n*Diplomacy and Alliances* A key aspect of Raymond III's rule was his skillful diplomacy. He sought to maintain a delicate balance of power among the various Crusader states and Muslim rulers. He formed alliances with different groups as needed to advance his interests. He engaged in negotiations with the Zengids and other Muslim powers seeking to avoid costly wars and maintain peaceful relations where possible. He also cultivated relationships with the Byzantine Empire seeking their support against common enemies. His diplomatic efforts were crucial in ensuring the survival and prosperity of the County of Tripoli. He understood the importance of political maneuvering in a region rife with conflict and shifting allegiances.\n\nRaymond III's reign saw significant economic growth in the County of Tripoli. The county thrived as a major trading center connecting Europe with the East. He fostered trade and encouraged the development of industries such as textiles and ceramics. He also invested in infrastructure projects such as roads and fortifications. His economic policies helped to improve the standard of living for the people of Tripoli and strengthened the county's position in the region.\n\n*Legacy and Death* Raymond III died in 1199 and was succeeded by his nephew Raymond IV. He is remembered as a capable and effective ruler who defended the County of Tripoli against numerous threats. He was a skilled military leader a shrewd diplomat and a successful administrator. His reign helped to maintain the independence and prosperity of the county during a difficult period. He left a legacy of stability and strength that benefited Tripoli for many years to come. His actions shaped the political landscape of the Levant and contributed to the complex history of the Crusades.\n\n\n\n", "logprobs": [{"token": "Raymond", "logprob": -0.0021344278939068317}, {"token": "▁III", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁Count", "logprob": -3.099436753473128e-06}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁Tripoli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁ruled", "logprob": -0.25026753544807434}, {"token": "▁the", "logprob": -0.5223273038864136}, {"token": "▁County", "logprob": -0.0855979174375534}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": "▁Tripoli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁from", "logprob": -0.000192504478036426}, {"token": "▁", "logprob": -2.5748875486897305e-05}, {"